## Downloading one tier at a time

Every folder is self-contained — its own data, docs, QA evidence and code. You never need more
than one.

**Easiest: download a single package archive.** From the
[Releases](../../releases) page, take just the tier you want:

```bash
tar -xzf pots-corpus-tier4-@@VERSION@@.tar.gz
cd pots-corpus-tier4-@@VERSION@@
shasum -a 256 -c CHECKSUMS.sha256          # confirm the download is intact
python code/verify_package.py --self-test  # confirm the data matches the docs
```

That's @@TIER4_ARCHIVE_MB@@ MB compressed for Tier 4, and nothing from the other four tiers is involved. No Git
needed.

**From Git, one folder only.** A plain `git clone` fetches all five (~@@REPO_MB_ROUNDED@@ MB working tree). To
get a single tier, use a sparse checkout:

```bash
git clone --filter=blob:none --no-checkout https://github.com/<you>/pots-corpus.git
cd pots-corpus
git sparse-checkout init --cone
git sparse-checkout set tier4
git checkout
```

You end up with `tier4/` plus the top-level docs, and none of the other tier folders. Add
another later with `git sparse-checkout add tier3`.

**Single file, no clone at all.** Every file has a direct URL, which is useful for a script
that only wants the table. Fetch the bytes, then read them:

```python
import io, urllib.request
import pandas as pd

url = ("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/<owner>/pots-corpus/main/"
       "tier4/data/tier4_findings.parquet")
df = pd.read_parquet(io.BytesIO(urllib.request.urlopen(url).read()))
```

Passing the URL straight to `pd.read_parquet(url)` also works, but only if `fsspec` and
`requests` are installed — they are not part of a default pandas install, so the two-step
version above is the portable one.

Replace `<owner>` with the account hosting the repository. Files over 100 MB will not serve
from `raw.githubusercontent.com`; nothing here approaches that (largest file: @@LARGEST_FILE_MB@@ MB).

